    "body": "## Problem\n\n`vim.mpack.encode()` picks the msgpack size of a negative integer from its low 32 bits only, so values below -2^32 can be written with too few bytes:\n\n```\nvim.mpack.decode(vim.mpack.encode(-4294967301))   --> -5\nvim.mpack.decode(vim.mpack.encode(-12884971888))  --> -70000\n```\n\nNumbers beyond ±2^53 hit the assertion in `mpack_pack_number()` in debug builds (#32216). In release builds, numbers below -2^63 go through an out-of-range conversion:\n\n```\nvim.mpack.decode(vim.mpack.encode(-2^63 - 2048))  --> -2048\nvim.mpack.decode(vim.mpack.encode(-1.5 * 2^63))   --> 4.6116860184273879e+18\n```\n\n## Solution\n\n- Use int64 whenever the high word is not a sign extension, in both `mpack_pack_number()` and the writer (`mpack_wnint()`).\n- Encode numbers outside [-2^63, 2^64), including NaN and infinities, as floats, and drop the assertion. Integers from 2^53 up to 2^64 are exact in a double, so they are still encoded as integers.\n\nFixes #32216\n",
